        }    

        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_BinarySearchContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(x));
	    
            // Sort the array list
            arrList.Sort();
	    
            int Old_arrList_IndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_BinarySearchRet = arrList.BinarySearch(x);
	    
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrList_IndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_BinarySearchRet", New_BinarySearchRet);
	    
            // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  if x in list @@@ then ret >= 0 
        }

    }
}


A: The code you've provided is for the ArrayList.Test class, which is actually a set of tests that validate some contracts associated with the ArrayList class.

Here's a quick rundown of what's happening:

1. PUT_AddContract: This test asserts that the Add method maintains the Count property accordingly, and that it retains the position of the item in the array.

2. PUT_RemoveContract: This test asserts that the state of the collection and its position of elements after a call to Remove method. It also asserts that the Remove method is correctly removing the item.

3. PUT_BinarySearchContract: This test case verifies that a specified value exists in an ArrayList, that the return result from BinarySearch is correct and that it returns index is greater than or equal to 0. 

It seems to me you are looking at some kind of contract testing code. It is a style of testing typically used in object-oriented programming where the goal is to ensure objects behave as expected and they adhere to certain pre-defined sets of conditions or properties. 

Just to make sure, have you downloaded the PexStudio plugin and installed it in Visual Studio ? It's necessary to be able to run Pex tests. Moreover, make sure that you have the latest version of the PexStudio plugin. If you still have problem, there are several resources available where you could get more help. You should be able to find good tutorials about using PexStudio with ArrayList in C#. 

Also, please note that these tests are not meant to be run on a project that uses ArrayList, as they're using the Microsoft.Pex.Framework. They're used to generate tests that can validate that the ArrayList.NET class behaves correctly.

I hope this clears things up a bit. If you have any more questions, just say so.

A: The issue lies in the way you are using the [PexMethod] attribute. The [PexMethod] is typically used to specify that a method is a method under test, but in the context of contracts, it could be used in a more abstract way, perhaps indicating that preconditions are met and that verification by the test is sufficient.

However, in a contract-based testing approach, your tests are checking invariants in the system - the properties that the system should maintain at all times. The Pex test runs different scenarios, but it's likely not exhaustive of all possible input, so it's not a complete test. If there's any property that it fails to meet in this set of tests, then it is likely not a real test suite, merely a verification-style test. A real contract test would require a wider range of test inputs to verify the contract is maintained over the lifetime of the objects.

Lastly, Pex may not be the best fit in your case. Pex is an exploration tool, and is generally used for the discovery of good tests, not for proving that a property holds always. The contract testing you're interested in could potentially be done by tools not only designed for exploration, but also capable of fine-grained verification. 

My strong suggestion is that you switch your approach to a traditional contract-based testing using a tool like QuickCheck or JML (Java Modeling Language). These languages and tools provide mathematical semantics to your code, meaning that you can specify in what conditions a component should behave and these validations can be exercised by automated tools. 

That would probably provide more meaningful and more robustness results. If you still insist on using contracts with ArrayList (which is quite legacy and far from modern design principles), you may consider looking at some contract testing frameworks like Concordion.

In any case, the--------------------	    Assert.True(New_arrListIndexOfX == -1);
	    Assert.True(New_arrListLastIndexOfX == -1);

        }

    }
