And PexAPIWrapper folder contains :
using System;
using System.Text;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Settings;

namespace PexAPIWrapper
{
    [PexClass(typeof(ArrayList))]
    [PexGenerateFromMembers("get_Item([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList instance, [PexPositiveValidValues(0, 1)] int index)")]
    public partial class ArrayListWrapper
    {
        public object get_Item(ArrayList instance, int index)
        {
            return instance[index];
        }
    }
}

When i run the test, i have this in the output window :
'ArrayList.Test.PUT_AddContract(ArrayListTest, arrList, 1)' failed
Error: Assertion failed
Condition: $arrList$arrList_Contains_x

And i can't understand why it fail, i have in the assertion value "$arrList$arrList_Contains_x" :


A: The assertion failed message indicates that your contract method (PUT_AddContract) tested the "Contains" condition for a value 'x' that is not added into an array, which would return false. 

PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

With these lines, you are indeed observing whether the old list contained the value 'x' (Old_arrListContainsX) and the new list contains the value 'x' (New_arrListContainsX). If you look at the output generated by Pex, you can check these values. 

Since you did: arrList.Add(x); which added the element 'x' into the array, the New_arrListContainsX should be 'true', as it returned an index greater or equal than zero. If it's 'false', it means the value 'x' wasn't added. Hence, you should investigate the Add method itself, to ensure its behavior matches your expectations. 

Make sure you've followed the expected behavior of the ArrayList.Add method while crafting the contract tests that ensure that it should always return the correct results when adding an item to the list.

In your current case, it appears that if 'x' was already in the list, it might have caused this assertion to fail. Hence, PUT_AddContract might be more a contract for ArrayList.Remove method. The contract for ArrayList.Add method should ensure to add values correctly.

If you're still having trouble, ensure you are using the most recent version of Pex (14.0.1501.1). Newer versions of Pex have bug fixes for common issue with Contract tests.

For more detailed understanding of the error and failing tests, you can utilize Pex Explorer. It would be able to display all details around the failure. 

Refer to these articles to understand more about Pex Explorer:
http://research.microsoft.com/en-us/um/cambridge/2007/10/03/pex-explorer-visual-studio-2008/
http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/hh859559.aspx.

Regarding your question about PUT_Remove_Contract failing, I'd suggest you to look into the implementation of the ArrayList.Remove method, and confirm that it behaves as expected, thus satisfying your contract.

Hope this would help you debugging your unit testing.
Good luck.

A: In method PUT_RemoveContract,
arrList.Remove(x);

It is assumed that x is present in the list before removing. 
The code then verifies if x is still in the list and fails if it still exists. 
In the case that x is not found in the list, the IndexOf function will return -1, in which case you are attempting to obtain the index using:
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
which is attempting to obtain the index of a non-existing element, causing an exception.

You should use a different method to test this case, ie:


*

*Remove value not in list

*Add a value to the list, remove it, and verify the count--------------------